Q: disable dark mode in django admin A: <p>Overriding the <code>dark-mode-vars</code> block with empty content will cause problems as you are disabling a JS file. This is a quick 'force light mode and hide the toggle' option.</p>
<pre><code>{% block dark-mode-vars %}
    {{ block.super }}
    &lt;script&gt;
        const mode = &quot;light&quot;;
        document.documentElement.dataset.theme = mode;
        localStorage.setItem(&quot;theme&quot;, mode);
    &lt;/script&gt;
{% endblock %}

{% block extrastyle %}
    &lt;style&gt;
        .theme-toggle {
            display: none;
        }
    &lt;/style&gt;
{% endblock %}
</code></pre>
<p>Add that to the admin <code>base_site.html</code> template. The ideal way of doing things? Probably not. Does it work well enough for now? Yes.</p>
<p>Don't edit the Django source files directly.</p>

Q: Why doesn&#39;t `scrollbar-gutter: stable` work on the `body` element? A: <p>The answer by Alohci is mostly correct, but here is some clarification that I needed when working through this.</p>
<p>If you want to add <code>overflow: hidden</code> on the <code>body</code> tag, but you want the scrollbar gutter to be applied, all you need to do is to apply <code>scrollbar-gutter</code> to the <code>html</code> tag.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
   scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}

body {
   overflow: hidden;
}
</code></pre>
<p>(Alohci's answer had <code>overflow: hidden</code> on the <code>html</code> tag, which was not necessary, and does not apply to what the OP was asking.)</p>
<p>In our case, we are setting the <code>overflow: hidden</code> to the body tag via JavaScript when an event is triggered.</p>
